update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('a0382964-136b-40dd-8ff0-c7ad7655832c'::uuid,'c73a02728970830b694d6c46075b3dc7','A is about sexual reproduction in bacteria. The argument never said life requires sexual reproduction, only some reproductive mechanism, and bacteria reproduce in other ways. A says nothing about whether something one-tenth the size of a bacterium can reproduce, so the size problem is untouched.'),
('fc820d3c-e52d-45d1-8d50-3d52873bdf95'::uuid,'fc0fa0bf9b95f62357e52dd8447e49a5','The evidence only works if each nanobe has to carry its own reproductive mechanism. B breaks that assumption: "Single-celled creatures can combine to form a multicelled structure and then reproduce." Apply that to nanobes. One nanobe may be too small, but a group could reproduce together, so being tiny no longer rules out life.

The move to repeat: when you can''t deny a premise, ask what the author assumed about how it applies, then find the answer that breaks the assumption. Here it was one individual, one mechanism.'),
('5d818801-84bc-45e1-b52e-d5959257cef8'::uuid,'d1d40ffbdabdf2878a410cc7dcc7e8ff','C gives us material phenomena about the size of nanobes, but only says some scientists "claim" they are fossilized bacteria. Something nanobe-sized that we knew was alive would weaken badly. A disputed fossil isn''t that, and it doesn''t show anything that small ever had a reproductive mechanism.'),
('7e6a1830-afe4-439b-bb1e-09465e9804d8'::uuid,'ce57f0a42e2123ae9adb85815729615e','D attacks old definitions of life built with "inferior microscopes no longer in use." The argument relies on one requirement, a reproductive mechanism, and D never says that requirement is wrong. The old definitions may have demanded the same thing. Nothing here helps a nanobe reproduce.'),
('ee8dfbe5-e812-4b20-ba36-087458e5095f'::uuid,'28eaff75cf30d0290c0f9bef23ebcafd','E says some animals are larger but simpler than others, so size and complexity don''t line up perfectly. It runs the wrong way for us: we need something tiny that is still complex enough to reproduce, not something big and simple. And one fact about lizards says nothing about the scale of nanobes.'),
('7267ba23-e0b6-4c08-82b5-b6a7bc036340'::uuid,'01b6aec9d421a4896356de5f13b14254','A says the recommended height differs for children and adults. The researcher already relied on that: the computers were "higher than recommended for children." A just explains why they count as too high, so it fits the evidence instead of challenging the jump to the same risk as office workers.'),
('6a6b61cb-d8d8-4b2c-b28d-34c4becdf41f'::uuid,'f488e32f441e3c19924ff664c72dfcfe','B is about time at home, but the conclusion is about the risk school computers create. More home use doesn''t make the school postures any less harmful, and home setups could be just as bad. The impact is unclear, so B can''t seriously weaken.'),
('d5fd00ef-8776-47b4-89e6-dc59ecabf247'::uuid,'6b78bdb2dfe0e002f415ce676a816cd7','The researcher moves from bad posture straight to the same risk as office workers. That only works if a child''s body reacts to strain the way an adult''s does. C denies it: children''s "greater suppleness" makes them "less susceptible than adults" to these injuries. Same postures, lower risk, so "the same risk" fails.

The move to repeat: when a conclusion says two groups are equal, look for a relevant difference between them.'),
('36c218a0-b435-4f75-9d5c-77b9b27d6cce'::uuid,'94a2a4a8c2020061443d64491060850f','D says office workers'' equipment is usually at the wrong height too. That makes the two groups look more alike, which helps the comparison. To weaken, we need a difference between children and office workers, and D supplies a similarity.'),
('a3d85147-0498-4315-a9af-66cf96041a74'::uuid,'213913c1e1f087708538c415ccb6ea91','E is about who reports injuries, not who gets them. Children might report fewer injuries simply because they don''t recognize them or don''t tell anyone. Reporting rates don''t show their actual risk is lower, and the conclusion is about risk.'),
('f6edadf6-60d5-48ce-9f90-cede1bf6c5a3'::uuid,'cb3652f3ddbfc9b9833dddf9231a5ac0','A says engineered plants offer few advantages over ordinary ones. That''s about benefits, and the scientist''s conclusion is only about risk. A plant can be pointless and perfectly safe. A gives no reason to think a one-gene change can do harm.'),
('e4c5265c-4434-4562-938d-c0d88273a7e3'::uuid,'89eff41ba76ffbd750b09378f301e30f','B says whatever risks exist "may be somewhat reduced by other factors" such as added vitamins and minerals. That makes the risk smaller, if anything, which fits the claim that "the risk is minimal." A weakener needs the risk to be bigger than the scientist thinks.'),
('4d40c543-17df-42e6-8d14-566aa99ab8ee'::uuid,'f9ce33f1778b04450778d4030018361b','C says scientists haven''t located the genes behind every trait. That is uncertainty, and it cuts both ways: the changed gene might matter or might not. It never shows a one-gene change can have a significant effect. D gives a known case; C only gives a gap in knowledge.'),
('19951a99-4f90-4c1c-8211-45dc8f710fe4'::uuid,'633f017f5c34f52b695fbcdfabcb6e46','The scientist''s key move is that a change this slight "cannot have effects significant enough to be worrisome." D gives a counterexample: plants whose toxicity "is known to be affected by the alteration of a single gene." D speaks of toxicity to "some animals," not specifically to humans, but that is still enough to break "cannot": one gene changed, and something as serious as toxicity changed with it. The size of a change doesn''t decide its effect; what the gene does decides it.

The move to repeat: when an argument says something is too small to matter, look for a case where something that small mattered a lot.'),
('a6b349da-7c52-4d0b-be95-85f7c3ecaa46'::uuid,'b50936c9eceade45968554caf1dedaaf','E attacks the opponents: the consumers most opposed tend to be ill-informed. Even if the critics are wrong about everything, the scientist''s own reasoning could still be bad. Undercutting the other side does nothing to the step from a slight change to no worrying effect.'),
('ab8446c4-0b25-48dd-a6f0-b8af498ad570'::uuid,'46862d4d7cdeb11bc7eb84917ad0c271','A is about the reverse trip: turning domesticated animals back into wild ones. The conclusion is only about breeding toward domesticity. Whether the trip back works says nothing about whether every species can make the trip forward.'),
('c00a699a-f633-4f26-ab6f-759634e49a6e'::uuid,'d6b1ba0b58a918709fd48267ea77d74c','B says wild members mate more often than tame ones in some species. That slows the program, but a breeder chooses which animals breed, so the tame ones can still be paired. The conclusion only claims domestication is possible "in principle," and B doesn''t make it impossible.'),
('d60f6eb9-593f-48cd-8344-d0afd8724180'::uuid,'e4bad5083fa6d7425942eb3cca9b1bca','The method only works if some individuals "display tameness when young." C removes that starting point for some species: "no members ever display tameness." No tame animals means nothing to select and nothing to breed, so those species can''t be domesticated this way, and "all animals" fails.

The move to repeat: when a conclusion says all, one counterexample weakens it. Find what the method requires, then look for a case where the requirement is missing.'),
('fd61136d-1740-4e90-9dee-861159b64f34'::uuid,'baa77c182b49246c274c9cfd6d23c9b6','D is the tempting one, since tame members being "less fertile" sounds like a real obstacle. But less fertile still means fertile. The tame animals still have offspring, so the program just takes longer, and the conclusion only says domestication is possible "in principle." D would work only if tame members couldn''t reproduce at all.'),
('b842bf1c-b79a-4aae-948d-3a7aa3828afd'::uuid,'78e3fb0a95222e7f7e586a8af2960c71','E is about species already domesticated: some members are tamer than others. That is variation after the process has succeeded. It tells us nothing about a wild species that has no tame members to start with.'),
('9bb3041a-a32f-4879-b791-28c06d0408c7'::uuid,'08885acf0a7366a621c0bbb83c9c7710','A says Dr. Faris knew medication A had no known sleep-inducing properties. That makes the statement look more like knowing deception, not less. It strengthens the ethicist''s case.'),
('29e12894-f4ee-43aa-bab0-20ed96bd5d13'::uuid,'1ed970797123c22dda833cb03300f41c','B says a committee is considering revisions to the code. The argument is about the code as it stands now. A possible future revision can''t change whether Dr. Faris broke the current rule.'),
('fdcbd4a7-5ec3-4c34-8756-386693c7f80a'::uuid,'6042ca8af1eadff14ca1f45322c8728c','The ethicist treats "no known sleep-inducing properties" as if it meant the drug couldn''t help anyone sleep. C pulls those apart: medication A "is a pain reliever that can indirectly lead to sleep" by easing discomfort. So telling the patient it would help them sleep was true, and a true statement isn''t deception. No deception, no violation.

The move to repeat: when an argument calls a statement deceptive or false, look for a way the statement could be true after all.'),
('8be65a8d-135f-418f-ab00-2fda0ae9d3c2'::uuid,'d9de37b7cdcd59325d0d7f3de7ce276d','D says other staff gave medication A to patients with trouble sleeping. Many people doing something doesn''t make it honest. If the drug can''t help with sleep, D only means more staff broke the code; it never gives a way the drug actually helps.'),
('2ad4ba2e-b9b0-42c4-9fa4-ffbf299d8f40'::uuid,'90d34f86ce94158a5b7c2dca0da7ae25','E rules out other medications as the reason the patient slept better. It''s tempting, because it seems to point back to medication A. But sleep can improve for many reasons besides medication, and E gives no way medication A could help. C supplies that mechanism; E doesn''t.'),
('e121ea5c-6f98-454c-b02d-eb3f65891491'::uuid,'bfb0845ec3ed7de3cab55ae938729e8e','A shows the plan backfires. The advocate wants every interaction listed to protect patients. But listing minor ones "would detract from a patient’s attention to serious interactions." The plan meant to protect patients would leave them less protected from the interactions that matter most.

The move to repeat: on a recommendation, a strong weakener is a cost that undercuts the plan''s own goal.'),
('dda1fcc5-ed53-403d-9cdf-fb01fe50241d'::uuid,'3591f95ff85cda105bdf1aafbb911dfb','B says many drugs have fewer documented interactions than aspirin. That means shorter lists for those drugs, but the requirement still applies to them. Fewer interactions makes notice easier, if anything, so B gives no reason to drop the plan.'),
('bdd9d97b-a74e-4994-a38e-d924c938cc46'::uuid,'94a2b7495bbf8fa99d83380f8cae3a83','C says notice of every interaction would cause "only negligible price increases for consumers." Cost was a possible objection to the plan, and C takes it away. That helps the advocate. A weakener needs a real downside, not the removal of one.'),
('5f88031f-93d6-4d6e-94cf-1df926d3304b'::uuid,'eb39c3185c38051cb5c1166a1dc481ea','D says many interactions haven''t been identified yet. The plan only covers "all known" interactions, so the unknown ones are outside it. D shows no harm from listing the known ones.'),
('51687569-98e5-4477-809f-82ca97513ddf'::uuid,'8a57a76bf2632c896f7329a615aba57f','E says pharmacists usually point patients to printed warnings. That suggests warnings actually get read, which makes notice look more useful. It strengthens the case for the plan.'),
('dc0721d8-671a-4b3a-8577-b11937f891e7'::uuid,'74b7f49b3052a7ed4095752530ef0295','A says the crackdown is part of a broad campaign against corporate misconduct. That describes the plan''s scope, not whether anything is being done. A broad campaign can still be doing nothing, and zero completed audits is just as unexplained.'),
('096597ca-3a98-4cc7-bbf9-405a0cefc89d'::uuid,'0dc2bb6d7ac996052637abab8538ef00','B is about personal income tax audits; the crackdown is about corporate income tax law. More personal audits shows the agency is busy, but not with this plan. If anything, it makes the lack of corporate audits look like a choice.'),
('6ac3ccd7-3216-4b3b-9fe5-1060bce18bae'::uuid,'35950ad2ae2e55d78789d656af9883e5','C says most corporate audits don''t turn up significant violations. That might make audits seem less worth doing, but it neither explains why none were completed nor shows any crackdown effort. The evidence of zero completed audits stands.'),
('14a6b671-83cd-4162-8d4e-e95b54ffe81b'::uuid,'609c7ab24fe56ae28bb300bc1ad62ae6','The argument assumes a real crackdown would show completed audits within a year. D says it "generally takes longer than one year to complete an audit." Now zero completed audits is exactly what we''d expect even if the agency began auditing the day it announced the plan. The evidence no longer points to inaction.

The move to repeat: when evidence counts results over a period, ask whether results could even arrive in that time.'),
('40fb9846-ab4d-4817-ac61-d25f4052f48a'::uuid,'3323de680221606887f494e883422618','E compares the last five years with the five before: fewer audits completed. That is a longer trend of doing less, which fits the idea that the agency isn''t serious. It doesn''t explain the past year''s zero, so it can''t weaken.'),
('330960d1-36bf-4034-86fa-e081dc3b96de'::uuid,'23a4028bdcba4f5e338c7303f4c5375f','A says strict judges sometimes preside over incorrect verdicts. The conclusion is about trials with obstructive behavior, and A is about trials without it. Wrong verdicts happening elsewhere doesn''t show that badgering witnesses is harmless.'),
('f26f51f3-7cd3-4c6a-89c9-335061a18f1b'::uuid,'923dbb3052018d7e5e97636e8a727d20','B says lawyers care less than judges about whether outcomes are just. That is about motives. The question is whether the tactics actually distort verdicts, and lawyers who care little about justice are, if anything, more likely to try.'),
('19a93fe6-3350-4f77-a976-f0d00fe3a012'::uuid,'02e4226d92ca320a96ecdc9967323ceb','C says people swayed by inflammatory language rarely admit it later. That makes the influence harder to detect, which gives us more reason to doubt verdicts, not less. It pushes toward the conclusion.'),
('fd1c9e7d-99fc-4f83-827c-20a7fb83adf0'::uuid,'2734bf535c20a1be09a50061f66a9ee8','The conclusion needs obstructive behavior to actually threaten the verdict. D says it "is seldom effective in cases where jurors are also presented with legitimate evidence." In a typical trial jurors do hear legitimate evidence, so the badgering usually changes nothing, and the mere fact that it happened is weak grounds for doubting the verdict.

The move to repeat: when an argument goes from a hindrance to a doubtful result, look for an answer showing the job still gets done.'),
('b8b4ba08-9ab2-461d-861d-80c2b05eb876'::uuid,'cd81e9f6fff9fb947545fc5c5d4eac0b','E says jurors are screened partly for bias. That happens before trial. A juror who starts out unbiased can still be swayed by badgering during it, so E doesn''t show the behavior is harmless.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;